---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfSecureSheetToPdf from '@/components/tools/pdf-convert/PdfSecureSheetToPdf.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="csv-to-pdf">
  <PdfSecureSheetToPdf client:load source="csv" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>Turning a data file into something printable</h2>
      <p>
        A CSV in a text editor is unreadable after about four columns. In a spreadsheet it is readable
        but not portable: the column widths depend on the machine. This page draws a real table with
        measured column widths, a repeated header row and consistent page layout, so the PDF looks the
        same for everyone who opens it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Typical uses: attaching a data export to a ticket or an email, printing a stock list or a
        delivery manifest, submitting a schedule to a portal that accepts only PDF, or archiving a query
        result in a format that will still open in ten years.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Delimiters are detected, not assumed</h2>
      <p>
        "CSV" is a family of formats. The parser checks the content and handles all the usual ones
        without a setting:
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Separator</th>
              <th scope="col">Where it comes from</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Comma</td>
              <td>The default in English-locale exports and almost all APIs</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Semicolon</td>
              <td>
                Excel in German, French, Spanish, Italian and Dutch locales, where the comma is the
                decimal separator
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Tab</td>
              <td>Data copied from a spreadsheet, and .tsv exports</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Pipe</td>
              <td>Legacy mainframe and telecoms exports</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The parser handles quoted fields that contain the separator, embedded newlines and doubled
        quotes, so a free-text column with commas does not shift your table.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Pagination and the repeated header</h2>
      <p>
        Rows continue onto as many pages as necessary. With the header option on, the first row of the
        file is drawn in bold with a shaded background at the top of each page, so a fifty-page export
        stays readable.
      </p>
      <p>
        Row heights come from the content, so a row with a long note wraps to up to four lines and the
        row grows to fit. Text longer than four lines is cut with an ellipsis, and the result shows how
        many cells were cut.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Layout recipes</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Your file</th>
              <th scope="col">Suggested setup</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>3–5 narrow columns, many rows</td>
              <td>A4 portrait, 10 pt, zebra on, grid off</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>8–15 columns</td>
              <td>A4 landscape, 7–8 pt, fit-to-width on</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>20+ columns</td>
              <td>A3 landscape, 5–6 pt, or split the file</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>A column of long free text</td>
              <td>Portrait, grid on, so the wrapped lines stay visually contained</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>A file for a printer's tray</td>
              <td>US Letter in North America, A4 everywhere else</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Encoding notes</h2>
      <p>
        Files are decoded as UTF-8, which modern exports use. Two things to know:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The tool handles a byte-order mark at the start of the file (Excel adds one when you use
          "Save as CSV UTF-8"), and it does not appear in the first cell.
        </li>
        <li>
          Legacy Windows-1252 files show replacement characters on accented letters. Save them as UTF-8
          first; all spreadsheet applications can do this.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The drawing step can render only characters in the built-in PDF fonts, which cover Western
        European alphabets. Cyrillic, Greek, Hebrew, Arabic and CJK text becomes a question mark, and
        the result reports the number of substitutions. The tool does not embed a font, because getting
        one would need a network request.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Large exports</h2>
      <p>
        There is no row limit and no file size limit, because there is no upload. A 50,000-row CSV
        renders to a few hundred pages in less than a minute on a laptop, with per-row progress and a
        working Cancel button. Memory is the only real limit, and the drawing is streamed row by row,
        not held as one large layout.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the PDF is too large to email, <a href="/compress-pdf-to-2mb">compress it to 2 MB</a>. For a
        text-only table, the lossless clean-up in <a href="/compress-pdf">Compress PDF</a> is usually
        better, because it keeps the text selectable.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/excel-to-pdf">Excel to PDF</a>: the same renderer for .xlsx workbooks.</li>
        <li><a href="/pdf-to-csv">PDF to CSV</a>: the reverse direction.</li>
        <li><a href="/csv-to-excel">CSV to Excel</a>: if you need a workbook.</li>
        <li><a href="/csv-to-json">CSV to JSON</a>: for an API or a script.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
